"""
Hand-written reading-practice sentences, one bank per JLPT level.

── Why this file exists ──────────────────────────────────────
Reading practice drew its sentences from the JMdict/Tatoeba examples
attached to each vocab word, filtered only by "does this sentence use
kanji above the level". Measured over ~650 candidate sentences per level
with study/difficulty's full gate (kanji + grammar + length):

    N5   24 of 666 usable   (3.6%)
    N4   61 of 642          (9.5%)
    N3  244 of 628          (39%)
    N2  288 of 568          (51%)
    N1  511 of 565          (90%)

Tatoeba is a bilingual corpus, not a syllabus. Its sentences are written
by adults for adults, so the further down the levels you go the less of
it is reachable -- and with 24 usable sentences the N5 picker had no
choice but to keep serving the ones that only LOOKED N5 because they
happened to be spelled in easy kanji (〜ようとする, 〜んです, and a
memorable one about an itch).

These are written for the syllabus instead. Every entry names the grammar
point it demonstrates, and the point is checked against the grammar
catalogue (content/grammar/*.json) by row_problems() rather than
trusted -- a sentence claiming a point it does not use would teach the
wrong thing to anyone reading the label.

── The rules each sentence follows ───────────────────────────
Enforced by tests/test_reading_sentences.py, which runs the whole bank
through row_problems() below:

  * every kanji is in the level's cumulative set
  * no grammar point above the level appears
  * every word the app's vocab deck knows is learnable at or below it
  * within the level's length cap (N5 26 chars, up to N1 80)
  * `grammar` names a real point AT that level, and the sentence
    verifiably contains it
  * `en` and `fr`, a translation in each language the app speaks

── Plan 192: and each sentence is AT its level ───────────────
Those rules only say what a sentence may not hold, and the bank they
kept was N5 sentences carrying one point apiece: 92% of the N2 rows
held no N2 word, and the breakdown a learner opens after reading one
tagged nearly every word N5. A reading run at N2 is for reading what
N2 is made of. So, for N4 to N1 (GRADED_LEVELS):

  * at least two deck words of the level itself besides the point's
    own words (LEVEL_WORDS_MIN), counted as the breakdown tags them
    (study/level_mix.words_at_level)
  * `focus` is one of them, so a rating schedules a card of the level
  * no word the deck does not teach (off_deck_words): a JMdict pool
    word has no level, and the course never teaches it -- nor one the
    vocab audit counts as having no card (deck_gaps)
  * no word in kanji the breakdown tags above the level (tagged_above),
    which difficulty.report's vocab gate lets through when the word
    splits into easier ones (問い合わせ, 引っ越し)
  * where the grammar detector has proved itself on the point
    (grammar_detect.can_find), it finds it, since the breakdown numbers
    the point for the learner
  * two sentences a point, in the catalogue's order

N5 is exempt from these: an N5 sentence's words are N5 or kana, and
its rows are pinned by the basics course (content/basics.json), 入門
and the ride.

── Writing a sentence ────────────────────────────────────────
  N4  one or two clauses of everyday life, in the kana the N4 kanji
      budget leaves, up to 34 characters
  N3  two clauses normal: situations, reasons, opinions, up to 46
  N2  society, work, the news, written register welcome, up to 60
  N1  editorial, essay, narrative, administration, up to 80

Natural Japanese first: a level word goes where a native writer would
put it, in a situation it belongs to, and never into a list of words
assembled around the level. The two sentences of a point differ in
situation and in words, and a level's sentences between them use the
level's vocabulary widely. Readings are part of the content: the
romaji the run marks a learner against is the tokenizer's, so a
sentence it misreads (方, 行った, 明日 as あす) is reworded, never left.
No sentence is copied from a published list or from the grammar
lessons' own examples. `python -m scripts.reading_bank_report --check
rows.json` runs candidate rows through every rule and prints each one's
furigana, romaji, detected points and words with their levels.

── Shape ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    {"jp": ..., "en": ..., "fr": ..., "grammar": "<pattern>", "focus": "<word>"}

`focus` is the word the sentence is built around -- reading.py reports it
as the phrase's headword so the "look this up" affordance has something
to look up, the same field a Tatoeba-sourced phrase gets from the vocab
entry it hung off -- and the card a rating of the sentence schedules.
Every table that remembers a sentence keys it by its text, so a row is
rewritten or removed without a migration.
"""
import hashlib

from content.grammar_points_data import GRAMMAR_POINTS_BY_LEVEL

